Example 1: A static method

java
public class Main {

    static void sayHi() {
        System.out.println("Hi!");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        sayHi();          // ✅ works — no object needed
    }
}

Output:

text
Hi!
You never wrote new Main(). Because sayHi is static.





Example 2: A NON-static method (needs an object)
java
public class Main {

    void sayHi() {                    // NO static
        System.out.println("Hi!");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        sayHi();                      // ❌ ERROR — needs an object
    }
}
To fix it, you must create an object:

java
public class Main {

    void sayHi() {
        System.out.println("Hi!");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Main m = new Main();          // create the object
        m.sayHi();                    // now it works
    }
}
So: no static → need new. static → no new needed.





Example 3: Real Java methods
Math.random() is static:

java
double x = Math.random();    // ✅ works — no "new Math()" needed
String.length() is NOT static:

java
String s = "Hello";
int n = s.length();          // ✅ you call it on a specific string
You can't write String.length() — Java says "which string?" You need an actual string first.

The side-by-side comparison
java
// STATIC — call it directly on the class
Math.random();        // ✅
Integer.parseInt("5");// ✅

// NON-STATIC — must make an object first
String s = "Hello";
s.length();           // ✅

// NON-STATIC — this would fail
Math m = new Math();  // ❌ Java doesn't even allow this
The rule
Keyword	How you use it
static	ClassName.method()
not static	object.method()
Math.random() → static → class name + method

s.length() → not static → object + method

Why main is static
When Java starts your program, no objects exist yet. So it needs a method it can call without any object. That's static.

If main weren't static, Java would say: "Okay, but... which object do I call it on?" — and there are none. Dead end.

That's the whole reason.

